{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8d6f331689813491", "type": "tool_result", "content": "500\tfinite template family changes surface wording without hallucinating conditions\n501\tor changing the underlying record.\n502\t\n503\tValidation and repair.\n504\t\n505\tThe PE output is validated against the template rules required by the proposed\n506\tmodel. The validation checks whether the user’s requirements are\n507\tpreserved, the output format is complete, characters are correctly matched to\n508\ttheir dialogue, sound events follow the intended order, and lyric and duration\n509\tconstraints are satisfied. If the rewritten result fails these checks, the\n510\tmodule performs a targeted repair using the reported validation errors. After\n511\tvalidation, the renderer returns the enhanced textual condition, including the\n512\testimated timeline, as input to the proposed model.\n513\t\n514\tTraining annotations, synthetic records, and PE outputs therefore use\n515\tcompatible condition formats. Section 5 describes how different conditional\n516\tviews are constructed from this format during training.\n517\t\n518\t5 Conditional Training for Rich-Timeline Audio\n519\t\n520\t5.1 Semantic Conditional Views\n521\t\n522\tRich-timeline training uses alternative condition views derived from the\n523\tstructured record in Equation 1 and the renderer in\n524\tSection 4.4. This section specifies which semantic\n525\tunits remain visible in each view.\n526\t\n527\tIn rich-timeline audio, independently dropping individual fields can leave\n528\tdialogue as the dominant explanation for the target audio and weaken the\n529\tcontrast supplied by scene conditions. We therefore apply dropout to structured\n530\tsemantic units before rendering:\n531\t\n532\tCM=𝒯​(𝒟M​(R)),M∈{full,speech,scene,∅}.C_{M}=\\mathcal{T}(\\mathcal{D}_{M}(R)),\\qquad M\\in\\{\\mathrm{full},\\mathrm{speech},\\mathrm{scene},\\varnothing\\}.\n533\t\n534\t(3)\n535\t\n536\tHere, 𝒟M\\mathcal{D}_{M} hides semantic units in RR before 𝒯\\mathcal{T} renders\n537\tthe condition. The full view retains every available condition. The speech view\n538\tretains dialogue, speech-side attributes, and the identity information needed\n539\tto interpret them. The scene view retains the persistent soundscape and\n540\tindependent sound events while removing dialogue and roles together. The empty\n541\tview is the unconditional condition. The mixture over these views is\n542\tconfigurable, but each selected view must remain an interpretable request for\n543\tthe target audio.\n544\t\n545\tFine-grained dropout follows the same rule. An independent event may lose its\n546\tdescription or timestamp only if the remaining row still provides an\n547\tinterpretable event description or localization; otherwise the complete event\n548\trow is removed. After any\n549\tdeletion, relations cannot refer to a removed event or source. Fields whose\n550\tsemantics cannot be separated safely remain in the full view. Conditional\n551\tdropout hides only condition content: it does not rewrite transcripts or lyrics,\n552\tpermute the true event order, or change the target waveform.\n553\t\n554\t5.2 Attribute Contrast and Role-Bundle Integrity\n555\t\n556\tAttribute contrast and long-context role binding place different constraints\n557\ton conditional dropout. We apply each rule only where the remaining condition\n558\tis semantically interpretable.\n559\t\n560\tAttribute contrast.\n561\t\n562\tAge, gender or voice class, accent, and dialect use a canonical attribute block\n563\twith an elevated and configurable missing probability relative to decorative\n564\tdetail. Across repeated draws, an independently interpretable attribute can be\n565\tpresent or absent for the same target audio. This contrast is intended to expose\n566\tthe attribute-specific conditional difference to CFG. Whenever an attribute is\n567\tvisible, it remains normalized, explicit, and unambiguous; the sampling\n568\tdistribution is configured to retain attribute-present examples as well as\n569\tattribute-absent draws.\n570\t\n571\tRole-bundle integrity.\n572\t\n573\tA rich-timeline role bundle that is referenced by visible dialogue cannot be\n574\tpartially deleted. Whenever a visible turn is labeled roleX, its role\n575\tcard retains every available core anchor: identity, gender or voice class, age\n576\twhen annotated, accent or dialect when annotated, and at least one timbre\n577\tanchor. Optional personality or lengthy descriptive prose may be dropped. The\n578\trole card and its dialogue turns remain atomically bound, so deleting a role\n579\tbundle also removes its linked turns from the visible condition. When reference\n580\taudio is present, the reference audio, its textual label, and the matching label\n581\tin the target dialogue remain bound.\n582\t\n583\tThus, independently interpretable attributes can participate in attribute\n584\tcontrast, whereas a role bundle already referenced by dialogue can only be\n585\tremoved together with its linked turns.\n586\t\n587\tCounterfactual construction in Section 4.3 creates a\n588\tnew valid condition–target pair; conditional dropout instead leaves the\n589\taudible content of the fixed pair unchanged.\n590\t\n591\t5.3 Classifier-Free Guidance\n592\t\n593\tThe semantic views in Section 5.1 provide the\n594\tvisible and empty conditions used for classifier-free guidance (CFG). At\n595\tinference, CFG combines the corresponding conditional and unconditional vector\n596\tfields as\n597\t\n598\tvcfg=vu+s​(vc−vu),v_{\\mathrm{cfg}}=v_{u}+s\\bigl(v_{c}-v_{u}\\bigr),\n599\t\n600\t(4)\n601\t\n602\twhere vcv_{c} is predicted from the visible condition, vuv_{u} is predicted from\n603\tthe empty condition, and ss is the guidance scale. The difference vc−vuv_{c}-v_{u}\n604\trepresents the proposed model’s learned response to information in the visible\n605\tcondition, and ss weights that difference. In this formulation, guidance relies\n606\ton contrasts learned from consistent views and does not itself supply role or\n607\tsoundscape information absent from the visible condition.\n608\t\n609\t5.4 Shared Continuous Audio VAE\n610\t\n611\tThe conditional mechanisms described above operate on a continuous audio\n612\tlatent space, which is defined for all supported domains by the shared VAE\n613\tdescribed here.\n614\t\n615\tDesign motivation.\n616\t\n617\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview covers speech, music, and general audio and\n618\ttherefore requires a shared latent representation that supports high-fidelity\n619\treconstruction across these audio domains. We compress 48 kHz stereo waveforms\n620\tinto a continuous latent sequence at 25 Hz, reducing the sequence length\n621\tprocessed by the DiT. We first train the VAE for high-quality reconstruction\n622\tand subsequently introduce auxiliary semantic supervision so that the latent\n623\trepresentation contains semantic information and is intended to better support\n624\tdownstream audio generation.\n625\t\n626\tLatent representation.\n627\t\n628\tFollowing the convolutional waveform autoencoder design of Stable Audio\n629\tOpen Evans et al. (2025), our VAE maps each waveform to a 128-dimensional\n630\tcontinuous latent sequence. The encoder parameterizes a diagonal Gaussian\n631\tposterior, and the decoder reconstructs the waveform from a sampled latent:\n632\t\n633\tqϕ​(𝐳∣𝐱)\\displaystyle q_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}\\mid\\mathbf{x})\n634\t=𝒩​(𝝁ϕ​(𝐱),diag⁡𝝈ϕ2​(𝐱)),\\displaystyle=\\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{x}),\\operatorname{diag}\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_{\\phi}^{2}(\\mathbf{x})\\right),\n635\t\n636\t(5)\n637\t\n638\t𝐱^\\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}\n639\t=Dθ​(𝐳),𝐳∼qϕ​(𝐳∣𝐱).\\displaystyle=D_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{z}),\\qquad\\mathbf{z}\\sim q_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}\\mid\\mathbf{x}).\n640\t\n641\tThe latent sample 𝐳\\mathbf{z} is used for waveform reconstruction, whereas the\n642\tposterior mean 𝝁ϕ​(𝐱)\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{x}) is used for semantic\n643\tsupervision.\n644\t\n645\tSemantic supervision.\n646\t\n647\tThe posterior mean is mapped by a lightweight projection module into the\n648\tembedding space of a frozen Qwen2.5-3B base language model. The projected audio\n649\trepresentations are combined with an instruction prompt, and the frozen\n650\tlanguage model supplies a next-token prediction objective over the target\n651\tresponse. Gradients update the VAE encoder and projection module, while the\n652\tlanguage-model parameters remain fixed. The language model is used only as an\n653\tauxiliary supervisor during VAE training and is absent from downstream audio\n654\tgeneration.\n655\t\n656\tTraining objective.\n657\t\n658\tThe VAE generator objective combines reconstruction, variational, adversarial,\n659\tfeature-matching, and semantic terms:\n660\t\n661\tℒG=ℒrec+λKL​ℒKL+𝕀adv​(λadv​ℒadv+λfm​ℒfm)+𝕀sem​λsem​ℒsem,\\mathcal{L}_{G}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KL}}+\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{adv}}\\left(\\lambda_{\\mathrm{adv}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adv}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{fm}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{fm}}\\right)+\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{sem}}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{sem}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{sem}},\n662\t\n663\t(6)\n664\t\n665\twhere ℒrec\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}} contains multi-resolution spectral\n666\treconstruction terms evaluated on stereo waveform representations,\n667\tℒKL\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KL}} regularizes the variational posterior, and\n668\tℒadv\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adv}} and ℒfm\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{fm}} denote the\n669\tadversarial and discriminator feature-matching objectives. The indicators\n670\t𝕀adv\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{adv}} and 𝕀sem\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{sem}} specify whether the\n671\tcorresponding objectives are active for the current training stage and sample.\n672\t\n673\tVAE training stages.\n674\t\n675\tWe adopt a progressive training strategy distinct from the proposed model’s\n676\tpre-training and post-training curriculum in\n677\tSection 4.1. The VAE is trained on approximately\n678\t200,000 hours of in-house audio data, predominantly consisting of music. We first optimize the waveform VAE with\n679\treconstruction, KL, adversarial, and feature-matching objectives to obtain a\n680\thigh-fidelity acoustic representation. Starting from this checkpoint, semantic continuation\n681\tadds the frozen language model and projection module. The discriminator is\n682\ttemporarily disabled during early continuation; reconstruction-only and\n683\tsemantically annotated samples are jointly used, with the semantic objective\n684\tapplied only to the latter. The discriminator is then reintroduced while the\n685\treconstruction and semantic objectives are jointly optimized. This schedule is\n686\tdesigned to incorporate semantic information while limiting degradation of the\n687\tacoustic representation learned during reconstruction training.\n688\t\n689\tSection 6 first evaluates the complete system on speech,\n690\tgeneral audio, and music, and then isolates the VAE through reconstruction\n691\tmeasurements and controlled downstream probes.\n692\t\n693\t6 Evaluation\n694\t\n695\t6.1 Text-to-Speech Generation\n696\t\n697\t6.1.1 Zero-Shot Speech Synthesis on Seed-TTS-Eval\n698\t\n699\tWe first evaluate zero-shot speech synthesis on Seed-TTS-Eval\n700\tAnastassiou et al. (2024), a widely adopted benchmark for assessing\n701\tthe intelligibility and speaker preservation of reference-conditioned\n702\tTTS systems. For each test instance, the proposed model is provided with a\n703\treference utterance and a target transcript, and is required to synthesize\n704\tthe target content while preserving the voice characteristics of the\n705\treference speaker.\n706\t\n707\tTable 2: \n708\tResults on the Seed-TTS-Eval benchmark.\n709\tWER/CER (%) measure content intelligibility, and SIM denotes WavLM\n710\tspeaker similarity on a [0,1][0,1] scale.\n711\tBold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results among the\n712\tevaluated models in each column, respectively.\n713\tA dash indicates that a result is unavailable or not applicable.\n714\tBeyond TTS marks support for audio generation, interaction, understanding, or\n715\tediting beyond conventional speech synthesis.\n716\t\n717\tModel\n718\tEN\n719\tZH\n720\tHard-ZH\n721\t\n722\tBeyond\n723\tTTS\n724\t\n725\tWER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n726\t\n727\tSIM ↑\\uparrow\n728\t\n729\tCER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n730\t\n731\tSIM ↑\\uparrow\n732\t\n733\tCER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n734\t\n735\tSIM ↑\\uparrow\n736\t\n737\tHuman\n738\t2.14\n739\t0.734\n740\t1.26\n741\t0.755\n742\t–\n743\t–\n744\t–\n745\t\n746\tAutoregressive Models\n747\t\n748\tSeed-TTS Anastassiou et al. (2024)\n749\t\n750\t2.25\n751\t0.762\n752\t1.12\n753\t0.796\n754\t7.59\n755\t0.776\n756\t–\n757\t\n758\tMing-UniAudio Yan et al. (2025)\n759\t\n760\t1.85\n761\t0.580\n762\t0.95\n763\t0.700\n764\t–\n765\t–\n766\t✓\\checkmark\n767\t\n768\tMiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct Team et al. (2025)\n769\t\n770\t5.37\n771\t–\n772\t1.96\n773\t–\n774\t14.14\n775\t–\n776\t✓\\checkmark\n777\t\n778\tUniMoE-Audio Liu et al. (2025b)\n779\t\n780\t1.90\n781\t0.560\n782\t0.80\n783\t0.650\n784\t–\n785\t–\n786\t✓\\checkmark\n787\t\n788\tStep-Audio-2-Mini Wu et al. (2025)\n789\t\n790\t3.18\n791\t–\n792\t2.13\n793\t–\n794\t16.31\n795\t–\n796\t✓\\checkmark\n797\t\n798\tQwen3.5-Omni-Plus Team (2026)\n799\t\n800\t1.26\n801\t–\n802\t0.99\n803\t–\n804\t–\n805\t–\n806\t✓\\checkmark\n807\t\n808\tVibeVoice-1.5B Peng et al. (2026)\n809\t\n810\t3.04\n811\t0.689\n812\t1.16\n813\t0.744\n814\t–\n815\t–\n816\t–\n817\t\n818\tFireRedTTS-2 Xie et al. (2025a)\n819\t\n820\t1.95\n821\t0.665\n822\t1.14\n823\t0.736\n824\t–\n825\t–\n826\t–\n827\t\n828\tQwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base\n829\tHu et al. (2026)\n830\t\n831\t1.24\n832\t–\n833\t0.77\n834\t–\n835\t–\n836\t–\n837\t–\n838\t\n839\tMiniMax-Speech\n840\tZhang et al. (2025)\n841\t\n842\t1.90\n843\t0.738\n844\t0.99\n845\t0.799\n846\t–\n847\t–\n848\t–\n849\t\n850\tVoxCPM2 Zhou et al. (2026)\n851\t\n852\t1.84\n853\t0.753\n854\t0.97\n855\t0.795\n856\t8.13\n857\t0.753\n858\t–\n859\t\n860\tCosyVoice3-1.5B\n861\tDu et al. (2025)\n862\t\n863\t2.21\n864\t0.720\n865\t1.12\n866\t0.781\n867\t5.83\n868\t0.758\n869\t–\n870\t\n871\tQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS Xiang et al. (2026)\n872\t\n873\t1.54\n874\t0.762\n875\t0.84\n876\t0.792\n877\t7.00\n878\t0.768\n879\t–\n880\t\n881\tNon-Autoregressive Models\n882\t\n883\tF5-TTS (32 NFE)\n884\tChen et al. (2025b)\n885\t\n886\t1.83\n887\t0.647\n888\t1.56\n889\t0.741\n890\t8.67\n891\t0.713\n892\t–\n893\t\n894\tF5R-TTS Sun et al. (2025)\n895\t\n896\t–\n897\t–\n898\t1.37\n899\t0.754\n900\t8.79\n901\t0.718\n902\t–\n903\t\n904\tLongCat-AudioDiT-3.5B\n905\tXin et al. (2026)\n906\t\n907\t1.50\n908\t0.786\n909\t1.09\n910\t0.818\n911\t6.04\n912\t0.797\n913\t–\n914\t\n915\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n916\t1.61\n917\t0.805\n918\t1.06\n919\t0.819\n920\t8.25\n921\t0.808\n922\t✓\\checkmark\n923\t\n924\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview records the highest SIM on the EN, ZH, and\n925\tHard-ZH subsets, making speaker preservation its clearest numerical strength\n926\ton this benchmark. Its WER/CER values are not the column minima, so the results\n927\tdo not establish a uniform advantage in linguistic accuracy. The proposed\n928\tmodel also supports the broader Beyond TTS task range, showing that a\n929\tunified generation scope is compatible with strong zero-shot speaker\n930\tsimilarity without implying leadership on every TTS metric.\n931\t\n932\t6.1.2 Reference-Conditioned Multi-Speaker Speech Generation\n933\t\n934\tSeed-TTS-Eval primarily evaluates reference-conditioned generation for\n935\ta single target speaker at a time. To assess the ability to jointly\n936\tcontrol multiple speakers, we construct an in-house benchmark for\n937\treference-conditioned multi-speaker speech generation.\n938\t\n939\tThe benchmark consists of 100 instances each in English and Chinese,\n940\tdrawn from the Seed-TTS test set Anastassiou et al. (2024), with 96 and 104 distinct\n941\treference speakers, respectively. Each instance contains KK reference\n942\tutterances from distinct speakers and a speaker-attributed target\n943\tscript {(si,ti)}i=1N\\{(s_{i},t_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{N}, where sis_{i} indicates the intended\n944\treference speaker and tit_{i} is the text of the ii-th turn. The proposed model\n945\tis required to render the entire dialogue as a single waveform while\n946\tassigning each turn to the correct reference voice and preserving\n947\tspeaker identity across turns.\n948\t\n949\tTable 3: Results on our reference-conditioned multi-speaker speech generation benchmark. WER and CER (%) measure content intelligibility over the complete dialogue waveform. SIM denotes WavLM speaker similarity between each generated segment and its reference audio. Consistency (CONS) denotes WavLM speaker similarity between segments of the same speaker across dialogue turns, reflecting voice preservation in multi-turn generation. Both similarity metrics use a [0,1][0,1] scale. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results in each column, respectively.\n950\t\n951\tModel\n952\tEN\n953\tZH\n954\t\n955\tWER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n956\t\n957\tSIM ↑\\uparrow\n958\t\n959\tCONS ↑\\uparrow\n960\t\n961\tCER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n962\t\n963\tSIM ↑\\uparrow\n964\t\n965\tCONS ↑\\uparrow\n966\t\n967\tSeed-Audio-1.0\n968\t1.20\n969\t0.575\n970\t0.659\n971\t1.35\n972\t0.661\n973\t0.704\n974\t\n975\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n976\t1.32\n977\t0.514\n978\t0.702\n979\t1.99\n980\t0.682\n981\t0.740\n982\t\n983\tCompared with Seed-Audio-1.0111https://docs.byteplus.com/en/docs/byteplusvoice/seedaudio-01,\n984\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview records higher CONS in both languages and\n985\thigher SIM in ZH. Seed-Audio-1.0 records lower EN WER and ZH CER, as well as\n986\thigher EN SIM. Thus, the proposed model’s consistent advantage across both\n987\tlanguages lies in cross-turn speaker consistency, rather than a uniform lead in\n988\tintelligibility or speaker fidelity.\n989\t\n990\t6.2 Text-to-Audio Generation\n991\t\n992\t6.2.1 Evaluation on AudioCaps\n993\t\n994\tWe evaluate text-to-audio generation on the AudioCaps test set\n995\tKim et al. (2019), using the publicly accessible OpenSound\n996\trelease, which contains 4,411 test caption instances.222https://huggingface.co/datasets/OpenSound/AudioCaps/viewer/default/test\n997\tBecause multiple audio realizations may satisfy a caption, we use complementary\n998\tautomatic alignment measures rather than direct waveform-reference metrics.\n999\tAll systems are evaluated using the same set of caption prompts. We report\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3e52b93c-ef55-4c97-94f9-0ca9b7cf4dde", "uuid": "52749ffc-c237-455c-a003-8d922294448b", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T08:10:33.353Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/qwen_audio_text.txt", "content": "finite template family changes surface wording without hallucinating conditions\nor changing the underlying record.\n\nValidation and repair.\n\nThe PE output is validated against the template rules required by the proposed\nmodel. The validation checks whether the user’s requirements are\npreserved, the output format is complete, characters are correctly matched to\ntheir dialogue, sound events follow the intended order, and lyric and duration\nconstraints are satisfied. If the rewritten result fails these checks, the\nmodule performs a targeted repair using the reported validation errors. After\nvalidation, the renderer returns the enhanced textual condition, including the\nestimated timeline, as input to the proposed model.\n\nTraining annotations, synthetic records, and PE outputs therefore use\ncompatible condition formats. Section 5 describes how different conditional\nviews are constructed from this format during training.\n\n5 Conditional Training for Rich-Timeline Audio\n\n5.1 Semantic Conditional Views\n\nRich-timeline training uses alternative condition views derived from the\nstructured record in Equation 1 and the renderer in\nSection 4.4. This section specifies which semantic\nunits remain visible in each view.\n\nIn rich-timeline audio, independently dropping individual fields can leave\ndialogue as the dominant explanation for the target audio and weaken the\ncontrast supplied by scene conditions. We therefore apply dropout to structured\nsemantic units before rendering:\n\nCM=𝒯​(𝒟M​(R)),M∈{full,speech,scene,∅}.C_{M}=\\mathcal{T}(\\mathcal{D}_{M}(R)),\\qquad M\\in\\{\\mathrm{full},\\mathrm{speech},\\mathrm{scene},\\varnothing\\}.\n\n(3)\n\nHere, 𝒟M\\mathcal{D}_{M} hides semantic units in RR before 𝒯\\mathcal{T} renders\nthe condition. The full view retains every available condition. The speech view\nretains dialogue, speech-side attributes, and the identity information needed\nto interpret them. The scene view retains the persistent soundscape and\nindependent sound events while removing dialogue and roles together. The empty\nview is the unconditional condition. The mixture over these views is\nconfigurable, but each selected view must remain an interpretable request for\nthe target audio.\n\nFine-grained dropout follows the same rule. An independent event may lose its\ndescription or timestamp only if the remaining row still provides an\ninterpretable event description or localization; otherwise the complete event\nrow is removed. After any\ndeletion, relations cannot refer to a removed event or source. Fields whose\nsemantics cannot be separated safely remain in the full view. Conditional\ndropout hides only condition content: it does not rewrite transcripts or lyrics,\npermute the true event order, or change the target waveform.\n\n5.2 Attribute Contrast and Role-Bundle Integrity\n\nAttribute contrast and long-context role binding place different constraints\non conditional dropout. We apply each rule only where the remaining condition\nis semantically interpretable.\n\nAttribute contrast.\n\nAge, gender or voice class, accent, and dialect use a canonical attribute block\nwith an elevated and configurable missing probability relative to decorative\ndetail. Across repeated draws, an independently interpretable attribute can be\npresent or absent for the same target audio. This contrast is intended to expose\nthe attribute-specific conditional difference to CFG. Whenever an attribute is\nvisible, it remains normalized, explicit, and unambiguous; the sampling\ndistribution is configured to retain attribute-present examples as well as\nattribute-absent draws.\n\nRole-bundle integrity.\n\nA rich-timeline role bundle that is referenced by visible dialogue cannot be\npartially deleted. Whenever a visible turn is labeled roleX, its role\ncard retains every available core anchor: identity, gender or voice class, age\nwhen annotated, accent or dialect when annotated, and at least one timbre\nanchor. Optional personality or lengthy descriptive prose may be dropped. The\nrole card and its dialogue turns remain atomically bound, so deleting a role\nbundle also removes its linked turns from the visible condition. When reference\naudio is present, the reference audio, its textual label, and the matching label\nin the target dialogue remain bound.\n\nThus, independently interpretable attributes can participate in attribute\ncontrast, whereas a role bundle already referenced by dialogue can only be\nremoved together with its linked turns.\n\nCounterfactual construction in Section 4.3 creates a\nnew valid condition–target pair; conditional dropout instead leaves the\naudible content of the fixed pair unchanged.\n\n5.3 Classifier-Free Guidance\n\nThe semantic views in Section 5.1 provide the\nvisible and empty conditions used for classifier-free guidance (CFG). At\ninference, CFG combines the corresponding conditional and unconditional vector\nfields as\n\nvcfg=vu+s​(vc−vu),v_{\\mathrm{cfg}}=v_{u}+s\\bigl(v_{c}-v_{u}\\bigr),\n\n(4)\n\nwhere vcv_{c} is predicted from the visible condition, vuv_{u} is predicted from\nthe empty condition, and ss is the guidance scale. The difference vc−vuv_{c}-v_{u}\nrepresents the proposed model’s learned response to information in the visible\ncondition, and ss weights that difference. In this formulation, guidance relies\non contrasts learned from consistent views and does not itself supply role or\nsoundscape information absent from the visible condition.\n\n5.4 Shared Continuous Audio VAE\n\nThe conditional mechanisms described above operate on a continuous audio\nlatent space, which is defined for all supported domains by the shared VAE\ndescribed here.\n\nDesign motivation.\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview covers speech, music, and general audio and\ntherefore requires a shared latent representation that supports high-fidelity\nreconstruction across these audio domains. We compress 48 kHz stereo waveforms\ninto a continuous latent sequence at 25 Hz, reducing the sequence length\nprocessed by the DiT. We first train the VAE for high-quality reconstruction\nand subsequently introduce auxiliary semantic supervision so that the latent\nrepresentation contains semantic information and is intended to better support\ndownstream audio generation.\n\nLatent representation.\n\nFollowing the convolutional waveform autoencoder design of Stable Audio\nOpen Evans et al. (2025), our VAE maps each waveform to a 128-dimensional\ncontinuous latent sequence. The encoder parameterizes a diagonal Gaussian\nposterior, and the decoder reconstructs the waveform from a sampled latent:\n\nqϕ​(𝐳∣𝐱)\\displaystyle q_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}\\mid\\mathbf{x})\n=𝒩​(𝝁ϕ​(𝐱),diag⁡𝝈ϕ2​(𝐱)),\\displaystyle=\\mathcal{N}\\!\\left(\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{x}),\\operatorname{diag}\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_{\\phi}^{2}(\\mathbf{x})\\right),\n\n(5)\n\n𝐱^\\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}\n=Dθ​(𝐳),𝐳∼qϕ​(𝐳∣𝐱).\\displaystyle=D_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{z}),\\qquad\\mathbf{z}\\sim q_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}\\mid\\mathbf{x}).\n\nThe latent sample 𝐳\\mathbf{z} is used for waveform reconstruction, whereas the\nposterior mean 𝝁ϕ​(𝐱)\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{x}) is used for semantic\nsupervision.\n\nSemantic supervision.\n\nThe posterior mean is mapped by a lightweight projection module into the\nembedding space of a frozen Qwen2.5-3B base language model. The projected audio\nrepresentations are combined with an instruction prompt, and the frozen\nlanguage model supplies a next-token prediction objective over the target\nresponse. Gradients update the VAE encoder and projection module, while the\nlanguage-model parameters remain fixed. The language model is used only as an\nauxiliary supervisor during VAE training and is absent from downstream audio\ngeneration.\n\nTraining objective.\n\nThe VAE generator objective combines reconstruction, variational, adversarial,\nfeature-matching, and semantic terms:\n\nℒG=ℒrec+λKL​ℒKL+𝕀adv​(λadv​ℒadv+λfm​ℒfm)+𝕀sem​λsem​ℒsem,\\mathcal{L}_{G}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KL}}+\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{adv}}\\left(\\lambda_{\\mathrm{adv}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adv}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{fm}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{fm}}\\right)+\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{sem}}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{sem}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{sem}},\n\n(6)\n\nwhere ℒrec\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}} contains multi-resolution spectral\nreconstruction terms evaluated on stereo waveform representations,\nℒKL\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KL}} regularizes the variational posterior, and\nℒadv\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adv}} and ℒfm\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{fm}} denote the\nadversarial and discriminator feature-matching objectives. The indicators\n𝕀adv\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{adv}} and 𝕀sem\\mathbb{I}_{\\mathrm{sem}} specify whether the\ncorresponding objectives are active for the current training stage and sample.\n\nVAE training stages.\n\nWe adopt a progressive training strategy distinct from the proposed model’s\npre-training and post-training curriculum in\nSection 4.1. The VAE is trained on approximately\n200,000 hours of in-house audio data, predominantly consisting of music. We first optimize the waveform VAE with\nreconstruction, KL, adversarial, and feature-matching objectives to obtain a\nhigh-fidelity acoustic representation. Starting from this checkpoint, semantic continuation\nadds the frozen language model and projection module. The discriminator is\ntemporarily disabled during early continuation; reconstruction-only and\nsemantically annotated samples are jointly used, with the semantic objective\napplied only to the latter. The discriminator is then reintroduced while the\nreconstruction and semantic objectives are jointly optimized. This schedule is\ndesigned to incorporate semantic information while limiting degradation of the\nacoustic representation learned during reconstruction training.\n\nSection 6 first evaluates the complete system on speech,\ngeneral audio, and music, and then isolates the VAE through reconstruction\nmeasurements and controlled downstream probes.\n\n6 Evaluation\n\n6.1 Text-to-Speech Generation\n\n6.1.1 Zero-Shot Speech Synthesis on Seed-TTS-Eval\n\nWe first evaluate zero-shot speech synthesis on Seed-TTS-Eval\nAnastassiou et al. (2024), a widely adopted benchmark for assessing\nthe intelligibility and speaker preservation of reference-conditioned\nTTS systems. For each test instance, the proposed model is provided with a\nreference utterance and a target transcript, and is required to synthesize\nthe target content while preserving the voice characteristics of the\nreference speaker.\n\nTable 2: \nResults on the Seed-TTS-Eval benchmark.\nWER/CER (%) measure content intelligibility, and SIM denotes WavLM\nspeaker similarity on a [0,1][0,1] scale.\nBold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results among the\nevaluated models in each column, respectively.\nA dash indicates that a result is unavailable or not applicable.\nBeyond TTS marks support for audio generation, interaction, understanding, or\nediting beyond conventional speech synthesis.\n\nModel\nEN\nZH\nHard-ZH\n\nBeyond\nTTS\n\nWER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n\nSIM ↑\\uparrow\n\nCER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n\nSIM ↑\\uparrow\n\nCER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n\nSIM ↑\\uparrow\n\nHuman\n2.14\n0.734\n1.26\n0.755\n–\n–\n–\n\nAutoregressive Models\n\nSeed-TTS Anastassiou et al. (2024)\n\n2.25\n0.762\n1.12\n0.796\n7.59\n0.776\n–\n\nMing-UniAudio Yan et al. (2025)\n\n1.85\n0.580\n0.95\n0.700\n–\n–\n✓\\checkmark\n\nMiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct Team et al. (2025)\n\n5.37\n–\n1.96\n–\n14.14\n–\n✓\\checkmark\n\nUniMoE-Audio Liu et al. (2025b)\n\n1.90\n0.560\n0.80\n0.650\n–\n–\n✓\\checkmark\n\nStep-Audio-2-Mini Wu et al. (2025)\n\n3.18\n–\n2.13\n–\n16.31\n–\n✓\\checkmark\n\nQwen3.5-Omni-Plus Team (2026)\n\n1.26\n–\n0.99\n–\n–\n–\n✓\\checkmark\n\nVibeVoice-1.5B Peng et al. (2026)\n\n3.04\n0.689\n1.16\n0.744\n–\n–\n–\n\nFireRedTTS-2 Xie et al. (2025a)\n\n1.95\n0.665\n1.14\n0.736\n–\n–\n–\n\nQwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base\nHu et al. (2026)\n\n1.24\n–\n0.77\n–\n–\n–\n–\n\nMiniMax-Speech\nZhang et al. (2025)\n\n1.90\n0.738\n0.99\n0.799\n–\n–\n–\n\nVoxCPM2 Zhou et al. (2026)\n\n1.84\n0.753\n0.97\n0.795\n8.13\n0.753\n–\n\nCosyVoice3-1.5B\nDu et al. (2025)\n\n2.21\n0.720\n1.12\n0.781\n5.83\n0.758\n–\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS Xiang et al. (2026)\n\n1.54\n0.762\n0.84\n0.792\n7.00\n0.768\n–\n\nNon-Autoregressive Models\n\nF5-TTS (32 NFE)\nChen et al. (2025b)\n\n1.83\n0.647\n1.56\n0.741\n8.67\n0.713\n–\n\nF5R-TTS Sun et al. (2025)\n\n–\n–\n1.37\n0.754\n8.79\n0.718\n–\n\nLongCat-AudioDiT-3.5B\nXin et al. (2026)\n\n1.50\n0.786\n1.09\n0.818\n6.04\n0.797\n–\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n1.61\n0.805\n1.06\n0.819\n8.25\n0.808\n✓\\checkmark\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview records the highest SIM on the EN, ZH, and\nHard-ZH subsets, making speaker preservation its clearest numerical strength\non this benchmark. Its WER/CER values are not the column minima, so the results\ndo not establish a uniform advantage in linguistic accuracy. The proposed\nmodel also supports the broader Beyond TTS task range, showing that a\nunified generation scope is compatible with strong zero-shot speaker\nsimilarity without implying leadership on every TTS metric.\n\n6.1.2 Reference-Conditioned Multi-Speaker Speech Generation\n\nSeed-TTS-Eval primarily evaluates reference-conditioned generation for\na single target speaker at a time. To assess the ability to jointly\ncontrol multiple speakers, we construct an in-house benchmark for\nreference-conditioned multi-speaker speech generation.\n\nThe benchmark consists of 100 instances each in English and Chinese,\ndrawn from the Seed-TTS test set Anastassiou et al. (2024), with 96 and 104 distinct\nreference speakers, respectively. Each instance contains KK reference\nutterances from distinct speakers and a speaker-attributed target\nscript {(si,ti)}i=1N\\{(s_{i},t_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{N}, where sis_{i} indicates the intended\nreference speaker and tit_{i} is the text of the ii-th turn. The proposed model\nis required to render the entire dialogue as a single waveform while\nassigning each turn to the correct reference voice and preserving\nspeaker identity across turns.\n\nTable 3: Results on our reference-conditioned multi-speaker speech generation benchmark. WER and CER (%) measure content intelligibility over the complete dialogue waveform. SIM denotes WavLM speaker similarity between each generated segment and its reference audio. Consistency (CONS) denotes WavLM speaker similarity between segments of the same speaker across dialogue turns, reflecting voice preservation in multi-turn generation. Both similarity metrics use a [0,1][0,1] scale. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results in each column, respectively.\n\nModel\nEN\nZH\n\nWER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n\nSIM ↑\\uparrow\n\nCONS ↑\\uparrow\n\nCER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n\nSIM ↑\\uparrow\n\nCONS ↑\\uparrow\n\nSeed-Audio-1.0\n1.20\n0.575\n0.659\n1.35\n0.661\n0.704\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n1.32\n0.514\n0.702\n1.99\n0.682\n0.740\n\nCompared with Seed-Audio-1.0111https://docs.byteplus.com/en/docs/byteplusvoice/seedaudio-01,\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview records higher CONS in both languages and\nhigher SIM in ZH. Seed-Audio-1.0 records lower EN WER and ZH CER, as well as\nhigher EN SIM. Thus, the proposed model’s consistent advantage across both\nlanguages lies in cross-turn speaker consistency, rather than a uniform lead in\nintelligibility or speaker fidelity.\n\n6.2 Text-to-Audio Generation\n\n6.2.1 Evaluation on AudioCaps\n\nWe evaluate text-to-audio generation on the AudioCaps test set\nKim et al. (2019), using the publicly accessible OpenSound\nrelease, which contains 4,411 test caption instances.222https://huggingface.co/datasets/OpenSound/AudioCaps/viewer/default/test\nBecause multiple audio realizations may satisfy a caption, we use complementary\nautomatic alignment measures rather than direct waveform-reference metrics.\nAll systems are evaluated using the same set of caption prompts. We report", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 500, "totalLines": 3041}}}
