en-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview leads the\n1154\tfull-set Qwen3 column and the subset Qwen3 and Qwen3.5 columns, ranks second\n1155\tunder Gemini, and does not lead any CLAP column. Its mean across the three\n1156\tsubset judges is 0.8373, compared with the next-highest mean of 0.8177 from\n1157\tUniFlow-Audio; its full-set and subset Qwen3 scores differ by 0.0018.\n1158\t\n1159\tThe CLAP and LALM results exhibit different system-level rankings,\n1160\treflecting their distinct evaluation mechanisms. At the individual-example\n1161\tlevel, the Spearman correlations between the three LALM judges and\n1162\tLAION-CLAP range from 0.136 to 0.254, while those with MS-CLAP range from\n1163\t0.037 to 0.091. These low correlations support treating the LALM judgments as\n1164\tcomplementary to, rather than replacements for, the established embedding-based\n1165\tCLAP measures: the former directly assess fulfillment of events, attributes,\n1166\tand temporal relations in the caption.\n1167\t\n1168\tTable 5 further evaluates perceptual quality\n1169\tusing AudioBox Tjandra et al. (2025).\n1170\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview records the highest values across all four\n1171\tdimensions. Taken together, the results locate its clearest numerical\n1172\tadvantages in the LALM and AudioBox assessments rather than CLAP, and do not\n1173\testablish a uniform advantage across evaluation mechanisms.\n1174\t\n1175\t6.2.2 Compositional and Temporal Instruction Following\n1176\t\n1177\tGlobal embedding-based metrics on benchmarks such as AudioCaps provide an\n1178\taggregate measure of audio–text alignment, but do not explicitly assess\n1179\twhether the requested events occur at the specified times. We therefore\n1180\tconstruct an in-house benchmark of 100 complex speech-and-sound scenes, with\n1181\tdurations ranging from 12 to 35 seconds, to evaluate event coverage and\n1182\ttemporal adherence. Each prompt specifies multiple speech and sound events\n1183\ttogether with their start and end times.\n1184\t\n1185\tFor each generated sample, Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview and\n1186\tQwen3.5-Omni-Plus independently determine whether each requested event is\n1187\taudible and estimate its temporal interval. We report the proportion of\n1188\trequested events judged audible (event recall), mean intersection over union\n1189\t(mIoU) between each reference event and its\n1190\tbest-matched predicted interval, and the proportions of events reaching IoU\n1191\tthresholds of 0.3 and 0.5 in Table 6.\n1192\t\n1193\tTable 6: Results on our in-house complex speech-and-sound benchmark for\n1194\tcompositional and temporal instruction following under Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview\n1195\tand Qwen3.5-Omni-Plus judges. All values are percentages. Bold and underlined\n1196\tvalues indicate the best and second-best results under each judge,\n1197\trespectively.\n1198\t\n1199\tModel\n1200\t\n1201\tRecall ↑\\uparrow\n1202\t\n1203\tmIoU ↑\\uparrow\n1204\t\n1205\tIoU@.3 ↑\\uparrow\n1206\t\n1207\tIoU@.5 ↑\\uparrow\n1208\t\n1209\tGemini-3.1-Pro-Preview as Judge\n1210\t\n1211\tSeed-Audio-1.0\n1212\t98.77\n1213\t38.48\n1214\t56.17\n1215\t38.27\n1216\t\n1217\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n1218\t88.58\n1219\t43.73\n1220\t66.36\n1221\t50.00\n1222\t\n1223\tQwen3.5-Omni-Plus as Judge\n1224\t\n1225\tSeed-Audio-1.0\n1226\t97.84\n1227\t37.36\n1228\t56.79\n1229\t36.73\n1230\t\n1231\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n1232\t87.35\n1233\t43.12\n1234\t65.74\n1235\t43.83\n1236\t\n1237\tBoth judges produce the same ranking pattern. Qwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n1238\trecords mIoU values of 43.73 and 43.12 under Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview and\n1239\tQwen3.5-Omni-Plus, respectively, compared with 38.48 and 37.36 for\n1240\tSeed-Audio-1.0. The proposed model also records higher rates at both IoU thresholds, whereas\n1241\tSeed-Audio-1.0 has higher event recall; this is a descriptive\n1242\tcoverage–localization trade-off, and no uncertainty estimates are reported.\n1243\t\n1244\t6.3 Text-to-Music Generation\n1245\t\n1246\tSongBench Wu et al. (2026) assesses seven complementary components:\n1247\tmelody, arrangement, and structure characterize composition and long-range\n1248\torganization; vocal, instrumental, and mixing assess source rendering and the\n1249\tintegration of musical elements; and musicality provides a holistic perceptual\n1250\tassessment. The in-house music model Dai et al. (2026) is a dedicated\n1251\tmusic pipeline whose music-token planner is an autoregressive (AR) hybrid\n1252\tlanguage model, whereas Qwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview is the unified\n1253\tnon-autoregressive (NAR) DiT described in Section 3.\n1254\t\n1255\tTable 7: SongBench Wu et al. (2026) component scores for base pre-trained\n1256\tcheckpoints evaluated on the same 20 examples. Relative music-data scales are\n1257\t1.0×\\times for the in-house model and 0.1×\\times for the proposed model. Parentheses give scores reported\n1258\tin Dai et al. (2026) for the final in-house system after reward-based\n1259\tpost-training, including RL, on a separate 500-example benchmark; these italic\n1260\tvalues are not directly comparable and are excluded from the base-checkpoint\n1261\tranking. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best\n1262\tbase-checkpoint scores in each component, respectively. Arr., Mus., Instr., and Struct. abbreviate\n1263\tarrangement, musicality, instrumental quality, and structure.\n1264\t\n1265\tModel\n1266\t\n1267\tSongBench ↑\\uparrow\n1268\t\n1269\tMelody\n1270\t\n1271\tArr.\n1272\t\n1273\tMus.\n1274\t\n1275\tVocal\n1276\t\n1277\tInstr.\n1278\t\n1279\tMixing\n1280\t\n1281\tStruct.\n1282\t\n1283\tIn-house music model\n1284\t[-1pt]\n1285\t\n1286\t5.750\n1287\t[-1pt](7.200)\n1288\t\n1289\t6.467\n1290\t[-1pt](7.388)\n1291\t\n1292\t4.548\n1293\t[-1pt](6.368)\n1294\t\n1295\t5.208\n1296\t[-1pt](7.623)\n1297\t\n1298\t6.448\n1299\t[-1pt](7.352)\n1300\t\n1301\t5.820\n1302\t[-1pt](7.305)\n1303\t\n1304\t5.425\n1305\t[-1pt](6.965)\n1306\t\n1307\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n1308\t\n1309\t5.608\n1310\t\n1311\t6.331\n1312\t\n1313\t4.813\n1314\t\n1315\t5.154\n1316\t\n1317\t6.559\n1318\t\n1319\t5.884\n1320\t\n1321\t5.322\n1322\t\n1323\tThe in-house model records higher values for melody, arrangement, vocal\n1324\tquality, and structure. Melody concerns continuity and development in the\n1325\tmusical line, while arrangement and structure concern how instrumental parts\n1326\tand song sections unfold over time. The higher values on these three components\n1327\tare consistent with the sequential and hierarchical planning bias of the AR\n1328\tmusic-token planner: stepwise prediction may help maintain dependencies among\n1329\tsuccessive musical tokens and longer song segments. The separate vocal-quality\n1330\tlead concerns singing and vocal rendering and is not attributed here to the\n1331\tsame planning hypothesis.\n1332\t\n1333\tThe proposed model records higher values for musicality, instrumental quality, and mixing.\n1334\tInstrumental quality concerns non-vocal musical rendering, while mixing\n1335\tconcerns the balance and integration of concurrently audible sources. The NAR\n1336\tDiT jointly refines a complete sequence of continuous VAE latents; the higher\n1337\tinstrumental-quality and mixing values are consistent with, but do not\n1338\testablish, the hypothesis that joint latent refinement can support the\n1339\tintegration of overlapping instruments, timbres, and levels. Musicality is a\n1340\tholistic and subjective component, so its higher score should not be attributed\n1341\tto a single architectural mechanism.\n1342\t\n1343\tThe comparison consequently yields no uniform ranking. With a checkpoint\n1344\tshared across speech, music, and sound effects and only 0.1×\\times the in-house\n1345\tmusic-data scale, the proposed model remains numerically close to the dedicated model in all\n1346\tseven components and leads in three. This result suggests\n1347\tcompetitive music capability for the proposed model at the evaluated data scale\n1348\tdespite its broader scope. It is not a controlled AR-versus-NAR ablation: the\n1349\tsystems also differ in training data, representations, objectives, and model\n1350\tscope. Moreover, the evaluation contains only 20 examples and reports no\n1351\tuncertainty estimates, so the differences should be interpreted descriptively.\n1352\t\n1353\t6.4 VAE Component Evaluation\n1354\t\n1355\tAfter the task-level evaluations above, we evaluate the VAE in\n1356\tSection 5.4 through waveform reconstruction and controlled\n1357\tdownstream probes. The probes train task-specific generators against different\n1358\ttarget representations; they do not evaluate the complete\n1359\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview system used in the preceding subsections.\n1360\t\n1361\tEvaluation setup.\n1362\t\n1363\tFor reconstruction, we compare DAC Kumar et al. (2023),\n1364\tEAR-VAE Wang et al. (2025a), SAME-L Parker et al. (2026), Stable Audio\n1365\tOpen Evans et al. (2025), Semantic-VAE Niu et al. (2025), LosaTok  Zhang et al. (2026) and\n1366\tHoliTok Li et al. (2026a). Speech is evaluated on Seed-TTS EN and\n1367\tZH Anastassiou et al. (2024), music on the Song Describer\n1368\tDataset Manco et al. (2023) and MuChin Wang et al. (2024), and general audio\n1369\ton AudioCaps Kim et al. (2019). We group representations by latent\n1370\tframe rate into High-Rate (≥40\\geq 40 Hz) and Low-Rate (<40<40 Hz) systems. In\n1371\tthe reconstruction tables, SR and LR denote waveform sample rate and latent\n1372\tframe rate.\n1373\t\n1374\tFor downstream probes, we use an F5-style conditional flow-matching\n1375\tgenerator Chen et al. (2025b) on LibriSpeech-PC-200 Panayotov et al. (2015)\n1376\tfor TTS and a UniFlow-Audio-style generator Xu et al. (2025b) on a 200-sample\n1377\ttext-to-music set. Within each task, every target representation uses the same\n1378\tgenerator architecture, training data, and optimization budget. We denote the\n1379\treconstruction-only checkpoint as Qwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic) and its\n1380\tsemantically continued counterpart as Qwen-Audio-Gen-VAE; Sem. indicates whether\n1381\tthe target representation uses semantic supervision.\n1382\t\n1383\tReconstruction quality.\n1384\t\n1385\tWe use ViSQOL for perceptual quality; Mel and STFT distances for spectral\n1386\tfidelity; SI-SDR for signal-level reconstruction; PESQ and STOI for speech\n1387\tquality and intelligibility; and CCPC and ICPC for stereo phase coherence.\n1388\t\n1389\tTable 8: Speech reconstruction on Seed-TTS EN and ZH. Bold and underlined\n1390\tvalues indicate the best and second-best Low-Rate results in each metric,\n1391\trespectively.\n1392\t\n1393\tModel\n1394\t\n1395\tSeed-TTS EN\n1396\t\n1397\tSR\n1398\t\n1399\tLR\n1400\t\n1401\tViSQOL ↑\\uparrow\n1402\t\n1403\tMel ↓\\downarrow\n1404\t\n1405\tSTFT ↓\\downarrow\n1406\t\n1407\tSI-SDR ↑\\uparrow\n1408\t\n1409\tPESQ ↑\\uparrow\n1410\t\n1411\tSTOI ↑\\uparrow\n1412\t\n1413\tHigh-Rate\n1414\t\n1415\tDAC\n1416\t\n1417\t44.1 kHz\n1418\t\n1419\t86 Hz\n1420\t\n1421\t4.3919\n1422\t\n1423\t0.4693\n1424\t\n1425\t1.0233\n1426\t\n1427\t11.2306\n1428\t\n1429\t3.7712\n1430\t\n1431\t0.9691\n1432\t\n1433\tEAR-VAE\n1434\t\n1435\t44.1 kHz\n1436\t\n1437\t43 Hz\n1438\t\n1439\t4.2253\n1440\t\n1441\t0.5532\n1442\t\n1443\t1.0599\n1444\t\n1445\t13.2584\n1446\t\n1447\t3.4765\n1448\t\n1449\t0.9660\n1450\t\n1451\tSAME-L\n1452\t\n1453\t44.1 kHz\n1454\t\n1455\t43 Hz\n1456\t\n1457\t3.9208\n1458\t\n1459\t0.6918\n1460\t\n1461\t1.2487\n1462\t\n1463\t13.4237\n1464\t\n1465\t2.7375\n1466\t\n1467\t0.9535\n1468\t\n1469\tSemantic-VAE\n1470\t\n1471\t16 kHz\n1472\t\n1473\t40 Hz\n1474\t\n1475\t3.2171\n1476\t\n1477\t0.7818\n1478\t\n1479\t1.7031\n1480\t\n1481\t9.6977\n1482\t\n1483\t3.6097\n1484\t\n1485\t0.9738\n1486\t\n1487\tLow-Rate\n1488\t\n1489\tHoliTok\n1490\t\n1491\t48 kHz\n1492\t\n1493\t25 Hz\n1494\t\n1495\t4.3905\n1496\t\n1497\t0.4248\n1498\t\n1499\t1.0050\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3e52b93c-ef55-4c97-94f9-0ca9b7cf4dde", "uuid": "aa20676e-ac4b-4a8b-9133-26d6b690a1ff", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T08:10:36.880Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/qwen_audio_text.txt", "content": "LAION-CLAP and MS-CLAP on the full test set to maintain comparability with\nestablished AudioCaps evaluations. We additionally employ\nQwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct Xu et al. (2025a) as an audio-language judge over the full set.\n\nTo provide a complementary cross-model evaluation, we uniformly sample\n100 prompts without replacement using a fixed random seed of 20260728.\nThe same prompts and generated audio samples are evaluated using both CLAP metrics\nand three independent large audio-language model (LALM) judges:\nQwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct, Qwen3.5-Omni-Plus333https://help.aliyun.com/en/model-studio/qwen-omni, and\nGemini-3.1-Pro-Preview444https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-3.1-pro-preview. The judges assess whether the generated audio\nsatisfies the events, attributes, and temporal relations specified by the\ninput caption. All baseline systems are reproduced following the settings\ndescribed in their original papers, and the reproduced CLAP results are\nconsistent with their reported performance.\n\nTable 4: \nResults on the AudioCaps benchmark. The full-set evaluation contains\n4,411 caption prompts, while the fixed random subset contains 100 prompts\nuniformly sampled without replacement using seed 20260728.\nQwen3 denotes Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct, Qwen3.5 denotes\nQwen3.5-Omni-Plus, and Gemini denotes Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview.\nAll LALM columns report mean audio–text semantic-alignment scores.\nBold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results in\neach column, respectively.\n\nModel\n\nFull Test Set (n=4,411n=4{,}411)\n\nFixed Random Subset (n=100n=100)\n\nCLAP ↑\\uparrow\n\nLALM ↑\\uparrow\n\nCLAP ↑\\uparrow\n\nLALM ↑\\uparrow\n\nLAION\nMS\nQwen3\nLAION\nMS\nQwen3\nQwen3.5\nGemini\n\nAudioX Tian et al. (2025)\n\n0.3208\n0.4042\n0.7832\n0.3137\n0.3980\n0.7995\n0.7000\n0.8400\n\nUniFlow-Audio Xu et al. (2025b)\n\n0.3712\n0.3924\n0.7467\n0.3765\n0.3852\n0.7245\n0.8100\n0.9185\n\nDasheng-AudioGen Mei et al. (2026)\n\n0.1969\n0.5086\n0.7327\n0.2003\n0.5053\n0.7325\n0.6790\n0.7625\n\nMMAudio Cheng et al. (2025)\n\n0.3612\n0.4317\n0.7095\n0.3618\n0.4416\n0.6775\n0.7215\n0.8375\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n0.3178\n0.3732\n0.8393\n0.3002\n0.3549\n0.8375\n0.8205\n0.8540\n\nTable 5: \nPerceptual-quality results on the AudioCaps benchmark evaluated using the\nAudioBox metrics PQ, PC, CE, and CU. All systems are evaluated on the same test\nprompts.\nBold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results in\neach column, respectively.\n\nModel\n\nPQ ↑\\uparrow\n\nPC ↑\\uparrow\n\nCE ↑\\uparrow\n\nCU ↑\\uparrow\n\nAudioX Tian et al. (2025)\n\n5.711\n3.196\n3.528\n4.969\n\nUniFlow-Audio Xu et al. (2025b)\n\n5.647\n3.125\n3.435\n4.923\n\nDasheng-AudioGen Mei et al. (2026)\n\n5.942\n2.948\n3.493\n5.180\n\nMMAudio Cheng et al. (2025)\n\n5.493\n2.989\n3.309\n4.867\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n6.256\n3.616\n3.960\n5.389\n\nIn Table 4, Qwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview leads the\nfull-set Qwen3 column and the subset Qwen3 and Qwen3.5 columns, ranks second\nunder Gemini, and does not lead any CLAP column. Its mean across the three\nsubset judges is 0.8373, compared with the next-highest mean of 0.8177 from\nUniFlow-Audio; its full-set and subset Qwen3 scores differ by 0.0018.\n\nThe CLAP and LALM results exhibit different system-level rankings,\nreflecting their distinct evaluation mechanisms. At the individual-example\nlevel, the Spearman correlations between the three LALM judges and\nLAION-CLAP range from 0.136 to 0.254, while those with MS-CLAP range from\n0.037 to 0.091. These low correlations support treating the LALM judgments as\ncomplementary to, rather than replacements for, the established embedding-based\nCLAP measures: the former directly assess fulfillment of events, attributes,\nand temporal relations in the caption.\n\nTable 5 further evaluates perceptual quality\nusing AudioBox Tjandra et al. (2025).\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview records the highest values across all four\ndimensions. Taken together, the results locate its clearest numerical\nadvantages in the LALM and AudioBox assessments rather than CLAP, and do not\nestablish a uniform advantage across evaluation mechanisms.\n\n6.2.2 Compositional and Temporal Instruction Following\n\nGlobal embedding-based metrics on benchmarks such as AudioCaps provide an\naggregate measure of audio–text alignment, but do not explicitly assess\nwhether the requested events occur at the specified times. We therefore\nconstruct an in-house benchmark of 100 complex speech-and-sound scenes, with\ndurations ranging from 12 to 35 seconds, to evaluate event coverage and\ntemporal adherence. Each prompt specifies multiple speech and sound events\ntogether with their start and end times.\n\nFor each generated sample, Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview and\nQwen3.5-Omni-Plus independently determine whether each requested event is\naudible and estimate its temporal interval. We report the proportion of\nrequested events judged audible (event recall), mean intersection over union\n(mIoU) between each reference event and its\nbest-matched predicted interval, and the proportions of events reaching IoU\nthresholds of 0.3 and 0.5 in Table 6.\n\nTable 6: Results on our in-house complex speech-and-sound benchmark for\ncompositional and temporal instruction following under Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview\nand Qwen3.5-Omni-Plus judges. All values are percentages. Bold and underlined\nvalues indicate the best and second-best results under each judge,\nrespectively.\n\nModel\n\nRecall ↑\\uparrow\n\nmIoU ↑\\uparrow\n\nIoU@.3 ↑\\uparrow\n\nIoU@.5 ↑\\uparrow\n\nGemini-3.1-Pro-Preview as Judge\n\nSeed-Audio-1.0\n98.77\n38.48\n56.17\n38.27\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n88.58\n43.73\n66.36\n50.00\n\nQwen3.5-Omni-Plus as Judge\n\nSeed-Audio-1.0\n97.84\n37.36\n56.79\n36.73\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n87.35\n43.12\n65.74\n43.83\n\nBoth judges produce the same ranking pattern. Qwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\nrecords mIoU values of 43.73 and 43.12 under Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview and\nQwen3.5-Omni-Plus, respectively, compared with 38.48 and 37.36 for\nSeed-Audio-1.0. The proposed model also records higher rates at both IoU thresholds, whereas\nSeed-Audio-1.0 has higher event recall; this is a descriptive\ncoverage–localization trade-off, and no uncertainty estimates are reported.\n\n6.3 Text-to-Music Generation\n\nSongBench Wu et al. (2026) assesses seven complementary components:\nmelody, arrangement, and structure characterize composition and long-range\norganization; vocal, instrumental, and mixing assess source rendering and the\nintegration of musical elements; and musicality provides a holistic perceptual\nassessment. The in-house music model Dai et al. (2026) is a dedicated\nmusic pipeline whose music-token planner is an autoregressive (AR) hybrid\nlanguage model, whereas Qwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview is the unified\nnon-autoregressive (NAR) DiT described in Section 3.\n\nTable 7: SongBench Wu et al. (2026) component scores for base pre-trained\ncheckpoints evaluated on the same 20 examples. Relative music-data scales are\n1.0×\\times for the in-house model and 0.1×\\times for the proposed model. Parentheses give scores reported\nin Dai et al. (2026) for the final in-house system after reward-based\npost-training, including RL, on a separate 500-example benchmark; these italic\nvalues are not directly comparable and are excluded from the base-checkpoint\nranking. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best\nbase-checkpoint scores in each component, respectively. Arr., Mus., Instr., and Struct. abbreviate\narrangement, musicality, instrumental quality, and structure.\n\nModel\n\nSongBench ↑\\uparrow\n\nMelody\n\nArr.\n\nMus.\n\nVocal\n\nInstr.\n\nMixing\n\nStruct.\n\nIn-house music model\n[-1pt]\n\n5.750\n[-1pt](7.200)\n\n6.467\n[-1pt](7.388)\n\n4.548\n[-1pt](6.368)\n\n5.208\n[-1pt](7.623)\n\n6.448\n[-1pt](7.352)\n\n5.820\n[-1pt](7.305)\n\n5.425\n[-1pt](6.965)\n\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview\n\n5.608\n\n6.331\n\n4.813\n\n5.154\n\n6.559\n\n5.884\n\n5.322\n\nThe in-house model records higher values for melody, arrangement, vocal\nquality, and structure. Melody concerns continuity and development in the\nmusical line, while arrangement and structure concern how instrumental parts\nand song sections unfold over time. The higher values on these three components\nare consistent with the sequential and hierarchical planning bias of the AR\nmusic-token planner: stepwise prediction may help maintain dependencies among\nsuccessive musical tokens and longer song segments. The separate vocal-quality\nlead concerns singing and vocal rendering and is not attributed here to the\nsame planning hypothesis.\n\nThe proposed model records higher values for musicality, instrumental quality, and mixing.\nInstrumental quality concerns non-vocal musical rendering, while mixing\nconcerns the balance and integration of concurrently audible sources. The NAR\nDiT jointly refines a complete sequence of continuous VAE latents; the higher\ninstrumental-quality and mixing values are consistent with, but do not\nestablish, the hypothesis that joint latent refinement can support the\nintegration of overlapping instruments, timbres, and levels. Musicality is a\nholistic and subjective component, so its higher score should not be attributed\nto a single architectural mechanism.\n\nThe comparison consequently yields no uniform ranking. With a checkpoint\nshared across speech, music, and sound effects and only 0.1×\\times the in-house\nmusic-data scale, the proposed model remains numerically close to the dedicated model in all\nseven components and leads in three. This result suggests\ncompetitive music capability for the proposed model at the evaluated data scale\ndespite its broader scope. It is not a controlled AR-versus-NAR ablation: the\nsystems also differ in training data, representations, objectives, and model\nscope. Moreover, the evaluation contains only 20 examples and reports no\nuncertainty estimates, so the differences should be interpreted descriptively.\n\n6.4 VAE Component Evaluation\n\nAfter the task-level evaluations above, we evaluate the VAE in\nSection 5.4 through waveform reconstruction and controlled\ndownstream probes. The probes train task-specific generators against different\ntarget representations; they do not evaluate the complete\nQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview system used in the preceding subsections.\n\nEvaluation setup.\n\nFor reconstruction, we compare DAC Kumar et al. (2023),\nEAR-VAE Wang et al. (2025a), SAME-L Parker et al. (2026), Stable Audio\nOpen Evans et al. (2025), Semantic-VAE Niu et al. (2025), LosaTok  Zhang et al. (2026) and\nHoliTok Li et al. (2026a). Speech is evaluated on Seed-TTS EN and\nZH Anastassiou et al. (2024), music on the Song Describer\nDataset Manco et al. (2023) and MuChin Wang et al. (2024), and general audio\non AudioCaps Kim et al. (2019). We group representations by latent\nframe rate into High-Rate (≥40\\geq 40 Hz) and Low-Rate (<40<40 Hz) systems. In\nthe reconstruction tables, SR and LR denote waveform sample rate and latent\nframe rate.\n\nFor downstream probes, we use an F5-style conditional flow-matching\ngenerator Chen et al. (2025b) on LibriSpeech-PC-200 Panayotov et al. (2015)\nfor TTS and a UniFlow-Audio-style generator Xu et al. (2025b) on a 200-sample\ntext-to-music set. Within each task, every target representation uses the same\ngenerator architecture, training data, and optimization budget. We denote the\nreconstruction-only checkpoint as Qwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic) and its\nsemantically continued counterpart as Qwen-Audio-Gen-VAE; Sem. indicates whether\nthe target representation uses semantic supervision.\n\nReconstruction quality.\n\nWe use ViSQOL for perceptual quality; Mel and STFT distances for spectral\nfidelity; SI-SDR for signal-level reconstruction; PESQ and STOI for speech\nquality and intelligibility; and CCPC and ICPC for stereo phase coherence.\n\nTable 8: Speech reconstruction on Seed-TTS EN and ZH. Bold and underlined\nvalues indicate the best and second-best Low-Rate results in each metric,\nrespectively.\n\nModel\n\nSeed-TTS EN\n\nSR\n\nLR\n\nViSQOL ↑\\uparrow\n\nMel ↓\\downarrow\n\nSTFT ↓\\downarrow\n\nSI-SDR ↑\\uparrow\n\nPESQ ↑\\uparrow\n\nSTOI ↑\\uparrow\n\nHigh-Rate\n\nDAC\n\n44.1 kHz\n\n86 Hz\n\n4.3919\n\n0.4693\n\n1.0233\n\n11.2306\n\n3.7712\n\n0.9691\n\nEAR-VAE\n\n44.1 kHz\n\n43 Hz\n\n4.2253\n\n0.5532\n\n1.0599\n\n13.2584\n\n3.4765\n\n0.9660\n\nSAME-L\n\n44.1 kHz\n\n43 Hz\n\n3.9208\n\n0.6918\n\n1.2487\n\n13.4237\n\n2.7375\n\n0.9535\n\nSemantic-VAE\n\n16 kHz\n\n40 Hz\n\n3.2171\n\n0.7818\n\n1.7031\n\n9.6977\n\n3.6097\n\n0.9738\n\nLow-Rate\n\nHoliTok\n\n48 kHz\n\n25 Hz\n\n4.3905\n\n0.4248\n\n1.0050", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1000, "totalLines": 3041}}}
