{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b49c71d275113627", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2000\t48 kHz\n2001\t\n2002\t25 Hz\n2003\t\n2004\t4.2828\n2005\t\n2006\t0.4778\n2007\t\n2008\t1.1315\n2009\t\n2010\t10.2356\n2011\t\n2012\t0.7785\n2013\t\n2014\t0.6729\n2015\t\n2016\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE\n2017\t\n2018\t48 kHz\n2019\t\n2020\t25 Hz\n2021\t\n2022\t4.3064\n2023\t\n2024\t0.5335\n2025\t\n2026\t1.2268\n2027\t\n2028\t9.9425\n2029\t\n2030\t0.7734\n2031\t\n2032\t0.6641\n2033\t\n2034\tTable 10: General-audio reconstruction on AudioCaps. Bold and underlined\n2035\tvalues indicate the best and second-best Low-Rate results in each metric,\n2036\trespectively.\n2037\t\n2038\tModel\n2039\t\n2040\tAudioCaps\n2041\t\n2042\tSR\n2043\t\n2044\tLR\n2045\t\n2046\tViSQOL ↑\\uparrow\n2047\t\n2048\tMel ↓\\downarrow\n2049\t\n2050\tSTFT ↓\\downarrow\n2051\t\n2052\tSI-SDR ↑\\uparrow\n2053\t\n2054\tHigh-Rate\n2055\t\n2056\tDAC\n2057\t\n2058\t44.1 kHz\n2059\t\n2060\t86 Hz\n2061\t\n2062\t4.2854\n2063\t\n2064\t0.5346\n2065\t\n2066\t1.1450\n2067\t\n2068\t5.9002\n2069\t\n2070\tEAR-VAE\n2071\t\n2072\t44.1 kHz\n2073\t\n2074\t43 Hz\n2075\t\n2076\t4.1789\n2077\t\n2078\t0.6078\n2079\t\n2080\t1.1402\n2081\t\n2082\t7.2334\n2083\t\n2084\tSAME-L\n2085\t\n2086\t44.1 kHz\n2087\t\n2088\t43 Hz\n2089\t\n2090\t3.9516\n2091\t\n2092\t0.7012\n2093\t\n2094\t1.3015\n2095\t\n2096\t7.6653\n2097\t\n2098\tLow-Rate\n2099\t\n2100\tLoSATok\n2101\t\n2102\t16 kHz\n2103\t\n2104\t25 Hz\n2105\t\n2106\t3.1208\n2107\t\n2108\t1.2495\n2109\t\n2110\t2.5070\n2111\t\n2112\t-34.7308\n2113\t\n2114\tStable Audio Open\n2115\t\n2116\t44.1 kHz\n2117\t\n2118\t21.5 Hz\n2119\t\n2120\t4.1664\n2121\t\n2122\t0.6731\n2123\t\n2124\t1.1796\n2125\t\n2126\t0.4401\n2127\t\n2128\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic)\n2129\t\n2130\t48 kHz\n2131\t\n2132\t25 Hz\n2133\t\n2134\t4.2538\n2135\t\n2136\t0.6075\n2137\t\n2138\t1.1708\n2139\t\n2140\t6.5950\n2141\t\n2142\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE\n2143\t\n2144\t48 kHz\n2145\t\n2146\t25 Hz\n2147\t\n2148\t4.3450\n2149\t\n2150\t0.6411\n2151\t\n2152\t1.1849\n2153\t\n2154\t6.4642\n2155\t\n2156\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic) achieves the best Low-Rate Mel and STFT distances on both\n2157\tmusic datasets and AudioCaps, while remaining competitive on speech despite\n2158\tits 25 Hz latent rate. HoliTok performs better on several speech metrics,\n2159\twhich is consistent with our music-dominated training corpus, where speech\n2160\taccounts for only a small fraction of the data.\n2161\t\n2162\tSemantic continuation improves ViSQOL on both speech sets, MuChin, and\n2163\tAudioCaps, while leaving the Song Describer result nearly unchanged. It\n2164\tintroduces modest degradation in spectral and stereo-coherence metrics, but\n2165\tlargely preserves the overall reconstruction quality.\n2166\t\n2167\tControlled downstream probes.\n2168\t\n2169\tTTS is evaluated with WER, speaker similarity (SIM), and UTMOS. Text-to-music\n2170\tis evaluated with OpenL3-FD, PaSST-KL, and four AudioBox\n2171\tscores: content enjoyment (AB-CE), production quality (AB-PQ), production\n2172\tcomplexity (AB-PC), and content usefulness (AB-CU).\n2173\t\n2174\tTable 11: Controlled TTS probe on LibriSpeech-PC-200 after 600k training\n2175\tsteps. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results\n2176\tin each column, respectively.\n2177\t\n2178\tTarget representation\n2179\tSem.\n2180\t\n2181\tWER (%) ↓\\downarrow\n2182\t\n2183\tSIM ↑\\uparrow\n2184\t\n2185\tUTMOS ↑\\uparrow\n2186\t\n2187\tMel spectrogram\n2188\tN\n2189\t19.69\n2190\t0.466\n2191\t1.769\n2192\t\n2193\tStable Audio Open\n2194\tN\n2195\t12.33\n2196\t0.504\n2197\t1.892\n2198\t\n2199\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic)\n2200\tN\n2201\t7.71\n2202\t0.507\n2203\t3.015\n2204\t\n2205\tSemantic-VAE\n2206\tY\n2207\t2.96\n2208\t0.594\n2209\t3.034\n2210\t\n2211\tLoSATok\n2212\tY\n2213\t3.30\n2214\t0.622\n2215\t3.411\n2216\t\n2217\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE\n2218\tY\n2219\t4.08\n2220\t0.488\n2221\t3.367\n2222\t\n2223\tTable 12: Controlled text-to-music probe after 150k training steps,\n2224\tevaluated on 200 samples. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and\n2225\tsecond-best results in each column, respectively.\n2226\t\n2227\tTarget representation\n2228\tSem.\n2229\t\n2230\tOpenL3-FD ↓\\downarrow\n2231\t\n2232\tPaSST-KL ↓\\downarrow\n2233\t\n2234\tAB-CE ↑\\uparrow\n2235\t\n2236\tAB-PQ ↑\\uparrow\n2237\t\n2238\tAB-PC ↑\\uparrow\n2239\t\n2240\tAB-CU ↑\\uparrow\n2241\t\n2242\tStable Audio Open\n2243\tN\n2244\t144.387\n2245\t1.103\n2246\t5.408\n2247\t6.240\n2248\t5.381\n2249\t6.250\n2250\t\n2251\tUniFlow-VAE\n2252\tN\n2253\t139.165\n2254\t1.058\n2255\t4.768\n2256\t5.569\n2257\t5.322\n2258\t5.563\n2259\t\n2260\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic)\n2261\tN\n2262\t140.333\n2263\t1.065\n2264\t5.959\n2265\t6.537\n2266\t5.063\n2267\t6.658\n2268\t\n2269\tLoSATok\n2270\tY\n2271\t119.792\n2272\t1.054\n2273\t6.269\n2274\t6.984\n2275\t4.901\n2276\t7.128\n2277\t\n2278\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE\n2279\tY\n2280\t118.954\n2281\t1.021\n2282\t6.397\n2283\t7.114\n2284\t5.260\n2285\t7.188\n2286\t\n2287\tQwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic) records better WER, SIM, and UTMOS than both the Mel\n2288\tspectrogram and Stable Audio Open targets.\n2289\tRelative to Qwen-Audio-Gen-VAE (Acoustic), semantic continuation lowers TTS WER and\n2290\traises UTMOS but reduces SIM; Semantic-VAE and LoSATok have lower WER and higher\n2291\tSIM than Qwen-Audio-Gen-VAE. In the text-to-music probe, Qwen-Audio-Gen-VAE improves all six\n2292\treported metrics relative to its acoustic checkpoint and records better values\n2293\tthan LoSATok, although Stable Audio Open has the highest AB-PC score. Because\n2294\tthe generator architecture, training data, and optimization budget are held\n2295\tfixed within each task, these differences concern target representations rather\n2296\tthan the proposed model as a whole.\n2297\t\n2298\t7 Conclusion\n2299\t\n2300\tQwen-Audio-3.0-Gen-Preview unifies speech, music, sound effects, and their\n2301\tmixtures through a single non-autoregressive generation path. Its shared textual\n2302\tinterface conditions a DiT in the continuous latent space of a common VAE, whose\n2303\t48 kHz stereo representation operates at 25 Hz and is augmented by semantic\n2304\tcontinuation after reconstruction training. The VAE decodes the generated\n2305\tlatent sequence directly into the complete mixed waveform. Real annotations,\n2306\tsynthetic recipes, and prompt-enhanced requests use compatible structured\n2307\trecords rendered as textual conditions, while semantic views, role-bundle\n2308\tintegrity, and classifier-free guidance expose complementary information\n2309\twithout breaking source–dialogue relations.\n2310\t\n2311\tAcross audio domains, the proposed model shows strong speaker similarity on Seed-TTS-Eval and\n2312\thigher cross-turn consistency than the compared system on the multi-speaker\n2313\tbenchmark. On\n2314\tAudioCaps, its clearest advantages occur under LALM and AudioBox assessments\n2315\twhile the temporal benchmark shows improved localization. Using 0.1×\\times the\n2316\tmusic-data scale of the dedicated in-house model, the proposed model remains\n2317\tclose across all seven SongBench components and leads in three. Reconstruction\n2318\tmeasurements and controlled probes support the utility of the low-frame-rate VAE\n2319\trepresentation. Together, these results highlight the potential for temporally\n2320\tcontrollable generation across audio domains, with data-efficient music\n2321\tcapability and no task-specific generation branches.\n2322\t\n2323\tAcknowledgements\n2324\t\n2325\tWe thank Zhifu Gao, Jingbei Li, Yiping Peng, Shiming Wang, Yang Xiang, Jianwei Yu,\n2326\tJixing Yu, Peiyun Zeng, and Nan Zhao for their contributions to data curation,\n2327\tsystem development, evaluation, and infrastructure support.\n2328\t\n2329\t(Names are listed alphabetically by family name.)\n2330\t\n2331\tReferences\n2332\t\n2333\t[1]\n2334\tA. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, et al. (2023)\n2335\t\n2336\tMusicLM: generating music from text.\n2337\t\n2338\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325.\n2339\t\n2340\tCited by: §1,\n2341\t§2.1.\n2342\t\n2343\t[2]\n2344\tP. Anastassiou, J. Chen, J. Chen, Y. Chen, Z. Chen, Z. Chen, J. Cong, L. Deng, C. Ding, L. Gao, M. Gong, P. Huang, Q. Huang, Z. Huang, Y. Huo, D. Jia, C. Li, F. Li, H. Li, J. Li, X. Li, X. Li, L. Liu, S. Liu, S. Liu, X. Liu, Y. Liu, Z. Liu, L. Lu, J. Pan, X. Wang, Y. Wang, Y. Wang, Z. Wei, J. Wu, C. Yao, Y. Yang, Y. Yi, J. Zhang, Q. Zhang, S. Zhang, W. Zhang, Y. Zhang, Z. Zhao, D. Zhong, and X. Zhuang (2024)\n2345\t\n2346\tSeed-TTS: a family of high-quality versatile speech generation models.\n2347\t\n2348\tExternal Links: 2406.02430,\n2349\tLink\n2350\t\n2351\tCited by: §6.1.1,\n2352\t§6.1.2,\n2353\t§6.4,\n2354\tTable 2.\n2355\t\n2356\t[3]\n2357\tS. Chen, C. Wang, Y. Wu, Z. Zhang, L. Zhou, S. Liu, Z. Chen, Y. Liu, H. Wang, J. Li, et al. (2025)\n2358\t\n2359\tNeural codec language models are zero-shot text to speech synthesizers.\n2360\t\n2361\tIEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech and Language Processing 33, pp. 705–718.\n2362\t\n2363\tCited by: §1,\n2364\t§2.1.\n2365\t\n2366\t[4]\n2367\tY. Chen, Z. Niu, Z. Ma, K. Deng, C. Wang, J. Zhao, K. Yu, and X. Chen (2025)\n2368\t\n2369\tF5-TTS: a fairytaler that fakes fluent and faithful speech with flow matching.\n2370\t\n2371\tIn Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers),\n2372\t\n2373\t pp. 6255–6271.\n2374\t\n2375\tCited by: §1,\n2376\t§2.1,\n2377\t§6.4,\n2378\tTable 2.\n2379\t\n2380\t[5]\n2381\tH. K. Cheng, M. Ishii, A. Hayakawa, T. Shibuya, A. Schwing, and Y. Mitsufuji (2025)\n2382\t\n2383\tMMAudio: taming multimodal joint training for high-quality video-to-audio synthesis.\n2384\t\n2385\tExternal Links: 2412.15322,\n2386\tLink\n2387\t\n2388\tCited by: Table 4,\n2389\tTab